---
// /docs/achievements: the conceptual home for the Product Achievements view.
//
// Hand-authored narrative (not a generated reference table), the sibling of
// /docs/conformance: conformance grades datasheet completeness, achievements
// report what a product is documented for. Both are computed views stamped
// into the generated index. Current-state only: this page describes what the
// standard IS, with no version-diff or changelog framing.
//
// The program-to-theme map itself lives upstream in the compliance and
// attestation glossary; this page explains the model, not the full mapping.

import PageShell from '../../components/PageShell.astro';
import SpecDocsLayout from '../../components/SpecDocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

interface AchievementRow {
  record: string;
  achievementsHtml: string;
}

const records: readonly AchievementRow[] = [
  {
    record: 'Cooper AtLite AUX (exit sign)',
    achievementsHtml:
      'emergency <code>documented</code>: its UL 924 listing is backed by two attached UL Certificates of Compliance',
  },
  {
    record: 'Cooper Sure-Lites ES (exit sign)',
    achievementsHtml: 'emergency <code>claimed</code>',
  },
  {
    record: 'Cooper Sure-Lites LPX (exit sign)',
    achievementsHtml:
      'emergency and energy <code>claimed</code> (UL 924; California Title 20)',
  },
  {
    record: 'Selux Aya',
    achievementsHtml:
      'dark sky, material health, and domestic content <code>claimed</code> (DarkSky Approved; Declare and Red List Approved; Buy American Act) · RoHS declared',
  },
  {
    record: 'Vode Nexa',
    achievementsHtml:
      'material health and domestic content <code>claimed</code> (Declare and Red List Approved; Buy American Act (BAA) and Build America, Buy America (BABA))',
  },
  {
    record: 'Lumenpulse Lumenfacade RGB and RGBW',
    achievementsHtml:
      'domestic content <code>claimed</code> (Buy American Act) · RoHS declared',
  },
  {
    record: 'ERCO Quintessence',
    achievementsHtml:
      'no themed programs (its ledger carries safety listings and test methods only)',
  },
];
---

<PageShell
  title="Achievements - ULC specification"
  description="The second computed view of a ULC record: per-theme third-party qualifications (embodied carbon, circularity, material health, energy, dark sky, emergency, domestic content), each none, claimed, or documented, computed from the record's attestations and stamped into the generated index."
>
  <SpecDocsLayout>
    <h1 class="page-h1">Product Achievements.</h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      Every ULC record carries two computed views, stamped side by side in its
      generated index. The <a href="/docs/conformance" class="cf-link">conformance
      level</a> answers "how complete is the datasheet?". Product Achievements
      answers a different question: "what is the product documented for?". Per
      theme, it reports the third-party program qualifications the record
      demonstrates, computed from the attestations the record already carries and
      stamped into <code>index.achievements</code>. Like the conformance level, it
      is computed, never declared: there is no place to assert an achievement by
      hand, and the two views never cross (a sparse datasheet can carry excellent
      sustainability evidence, and a lab-characterized fixture can carry none).
    </p>

    {/* ── Seven themes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cf-section">
      <h2 class="cf-h2">Seven themes</h2>
      <p>
        An attestation contributes to a theme through its program token. Seven
        themes are computed:
      </p>
      <dl class="cf-levels">
        <dt>Embodied carbon</dt>
        <dd>
          Environmental Product Declarations (ISO 14025) and TM-65.2
          embodied-carbon assessments.
        </dd>
        <dt>Circularity</dt>
        <dd>
          TM-66 assured circular-economy assessments and Cradle to Cradle
          certification.
        </dd>
        <dt>Material health</dt>
        <dd>
          Declare labels, Living Building Challenge Red List statuses, Health
          Product Declarations, and Cradle to Cradle (which contributes to both
          circularity and material health).
        </dd>
        <dt>Energy</dt>
        <dd>
          DLC listings, ENERGY STAR, California Title 20 and JA8, the EU ecodesign
          and energy-label programs, and NRCan efficiency regulations.
        </dd>
        <dt>Dark sky</dt>
        <dd>DarkSky Approved and DLC LUNA.</dd>
        <dt>Emergency</dt>
        <dd>
          UL 924, UL 1994, and the ICEL emergency-lighting conformity scheme.
        </dd>
        <dt>Domestic content</dt>
        <dd>
          The US federal-procurement programs: the Buy American Act (BAA), Build
          America, Buy America (BABA), and American Iron and Steel (AIS).
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
        A record's sustainability declaration contributes too: a Declare or Red
        List declaration feeds material health, and a manufacturer recycle program
        feeds circularity, each capped at <code>claimed</code>, since a
        declaration carries no evidence document.
      </p>
      <p>
        The full program-to-theme map is published as a versioned appendix in the
        <a
          href="https://github.com/ulcspec/ULC/blob/main/docs/compliance-attestation.md#achievement-themes"
          class="cf-link"
          rel="noopener external"
          >compliance and attestation glossary</a
        >. Programs that certify projects or companies (LEED, WELL),
        multi-attribute ecolabels (EPEAT, UL ECOLOGO), and the controls programs
        held for a future theme are deliberately unthemed. Two origin tokens are
        permanently unthemed by design: Trade Agreements Act eligibility can be
        satisfied by non-US manufacture, and country of origin is a neutral
        provenance fact, not an achievement.
      </p>
      <p class="cf-note">
        Domestic-content eligibility on a given project is confirmed per project
        by the manufacturer, not by this badge. ULC reports what the record
        carries; it does not certify that a product qualifies for a specific
        contract.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── Three states, one evidence gate ───────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cf-section">
      <h2 class="cf-h2">Three states, one evidence gate</h2>
      <dl class="cf-levels">
        <dt><code>none</code></dt>
        <dd>No qualifying attestation or declaration contributes to the theme.</dd>
        <dt><code>claimed</code></dt>
        <dd>
          A qualifying attestation or declaration exists, but no attached, current
          evidence document backs it.
        </dd>
        <dt><code>documented</code></dt>
        <dd>
          At least one qualifying attestation carries an attached evidence
          document: a file reference with a filename and a SHA-256 content hash,
          unexpired as of the record's own as-of date.
        </dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
        The discriminator is evidence, never a status label: attaching the
        certificate file is what raises a theme from <code>claimed</code> to
        <code>documented</code>. Expiry is judged against the record's own
        <code>record_status_as_of</code> date, never the build clock, so the same
        record computes the same achievements today and next year. An attestation
        whose status is expired, withdrawn, or not applicable contributes nothing
        at all.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── No overall score ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cf-section">
      <h2 class="cf-h2">No overall score</h2>
      <p>
        There is no weighted or overall achievement grade. The only rollup is
        <code>documented_count</code>, a factual count of themes at
        <code>documented</code>. Weighting embodied carbon against material health
        has no accepted method, and inventing one would be ULC editorializing.
      </p>
      <p class="cf-note">
        <code>documented</code> is a record fact, never a verification: it means an
        evidence document is attached to the record, not that ULC verified the
        underlying program. The verification standing of a claim lives in the
        attestation's own status. This is the same trust boundary the conformance
        level observes: ULC grades what a record carries; it does not certify
        products.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── The legal floor: restricted substances ────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cf-section">
      <h2 class="cf-h2">The legal floor: restricted substances</h2>
      <p>
        Beside the themes, never inside them, the index carries
        <code>restricted_substances_declared</code>: the RoHS, REACH, Prop 65, and
        similar restricted-substances programs declared anywhere in the record.
        Restricted-substances compliance is a legal floor, not a prestige
        achievement, so it surfaces as a flag rather than a theme.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── On the reference records ──────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cf-section">
      <h2 class="cf-h2">On the reference records</h2>
      <div class="cf-table-wrap">
        <table class="cf-table cf-table--ach">
          <colgroup>
            <col class="ach-col-record" />
            <col class="ach-col-ach" />
          </colgroup>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Record</th>
              <th scope="col">Achievements</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {records.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{r.record}</th>
                <td data-label="Achievements" set:html={r.achievementsHtml}></td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="cf-note">
        Two of the Cooper signs are the axis in one comparison: same program,
        different evidence. The AtLite AUX record has its certificates attached and
        reads <code>documented</code>; the Sure-Lites ES record carries a UL 924
        listing of its own with no attached document and reads <code>claimed</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ── See it on your own records ────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="cf-section">
      <h2 class="cf-h2">See it on your own records</h2>
      <Callout intent="accent" label="Run the reference validator">
        <p>
          <code>ulc validate &lt;record&gt;</code> prints a one-line summary by
          default: <code>achievements: N documented, M claimed</code>. Add
          <code>--verbose</code> (or <code>--json</code>) for the per-theme detail
          and the roadmap: which themes are <code>claimed</code>, and which
          evidence attachment would raise each to <code>documented</code>. An
          opt-in <code>--expiry</code> flag adds a report-only advisory on top:
          which dated claims have lapsed, and which themes a re-stamp would drop
          from <code>documented</code> to <code>claimed</code>. It never fails
          validation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The validator is a free download on the
          <a href="/downloads#cli" class="cf-link">downloads page</a>. How the
          conformance level is computed is at
          <a href="/docs/conformance" class="cf-link">/docs/conformance</a>.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </section>
  </SpecDocsLayout>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .page-h1 {
